\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Kimodo-Policy adapts a pretrained text-to-motion diffusion model into a visually grounded vision-language-action policy for humanoid loco-manipulation. By combining hierarchical root--body--hand control with visual ControlNet conditioning and motion-history context, the method produces coordinated whole-body behavior while preserving the structure of the pretrained motion prior. Across HumanoidArena, SIMPLE, and real-world evaluations, the policy achieves strong task success and low fall rates, benefits from larger VLA policy-pretraining corpora and VLA-pretrained initialization, and supports both single-task specialization and multi-task control. These results show that structured motion priors can provide an effective foundation for language-conditioned humanoid control.

\textbf{Limitations.} The current evaluation remains limited in task diversity, environmental variation, and the number of physical-robot trials. Performance is task-dependent, with some manipulation and distribution-shifted tasks still lagging behind specialized baselines, and several SIMPLE results remain pending. The scaling and ablation studies also cover a limited set of model sizes and training conditions, while the current policy relies on a fixed visual encoder, predefined motion representations, and simulator or demonstration data whose coverage may not reflect the full range of real-world interactions.

\textbf{Future work.} Future work will expand the motion and demonstration datasets to cover more diverse objects, contacts, terrain, and long-horizon language instructions, while improving visual grounding and closed-loop adaptation under changing environments. We also plan to develop more efficient policy variants and investigate uncertainty-aware control, online adaptation, and stronger sim-to-real transfer. Broader physical-robot evaluations with standardized protocols will further test the reliability, generalization, and safety of Kimodo-Policy beyond the current benchmarks.
